  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Asthma, well controlled (J45.30)</content>. Well controlled on as-needed budesonide-formoterol (Asthma Control Test 21), with no exacerbation. Step 2 is the right step; there is no reason to add maintenance treatment before the journey. Because she will be at sea for weeks, far from medical care, she needs enough medicine, a clear plan and a standby course. [S]<list><item>Two inhalers and the spacer in her cabin bag, more than enough for the summer.</item><item>Action plan updated with zones from her best of 400 L/min, and instructions for the standby course.</item><item>Standby prednisone 40 mg daily for five days, to start only if peak flow falls below 200 L/min, or below 320 L/min without improvement after 48 hours of extra reliever, and then to seek the ship&apos;s doctor or a local clinic.</item><item>Meclizine 25 mg for seasickness; avoid smoke-filled cabins and keep the cabin aired against damp.</item><item>Contact the clinic by message from abroad; printed medical summary.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Prone to seasickness (T75.3XXA)</content>. Likely at sea for a long crossing; meclizine is suitable and does not affect her asthma treatment. [S]<list><item>Meclizine 25 mg an hour before sailing and then once daily as needed; watch the horizon, stay on deck in fresh air when she can, and avoid reading in the first days at sea.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Asthma Control Test, symptoms, night waking and reliever use</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Asthma Control Test 21; reliever two or three times a week; no night waking</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Peak flow personal best</td><td>After starting treatment, to set action plan zones</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Best 400 L/min from the diary; 395 in clinic</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Inhaler technique with her own inhaler, and adherence</td><td>Every visit; before any step-up</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Correct with the spacer</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Written asthma action plan</td><td>Every visit and after every exacerbation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Zones set from her best; travel instructions added</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exacerbation history (oral corticosteroid courses, emergency visits, admissions)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Step-up or step-down assessment (GINA track 1)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Controlled at step 2; no change</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Triggers, exposures and smoking or vaping</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Window and fan when cooking; avoids fireplace smoke; scarf in the wind</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Travel planning</td><td>Before any long journey away from care</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Medicine supply, standby course, updated plan, medical summary and seasickness treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Repeat spirometry</td><td>Three to six months after starting treatment</td><td>Due</td><td>After her return in the autumn</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Private conversation: consent, safety and exploitation, and her own decisions</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen alone; she will travel as a member of a party from the household, and she alone decides on her own care abroad</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>A confirmed diagnosis within two weeks: spirometry with bronchodilator response and FeNO, with tuberculosis excluded. Outcome by 1773-02-23: Asthma confirmed by a 330 mL (15 percent) bronchodilator response and FeNO 38 ppb; tuberculosis test negative and chest X-ray clear, two weeks after the first visit [S]</item><item>Asthma Control Test 20 or more with no night waking. Outcome by 1773-04-06: Asthma Control Test 21 and no night waking six weeks after starting treatment; 20 after the exacerbation [S]</item><item>Correct inhaler technique and a written action plan she can use at home and at sea. Outcome by 1773-04-06: Technique correct at every visit; plan used during the exacerbation [S]</item><item>Well enough to make the sea journey and spend the summer away. In progress; target 1773-09-21 [S]</item><item>No exacerbation needing an oral corticosteroid. In progress; target 1773-10-26 [S]</item><item>Hepatitis B and HPV vaccination complete. In progress; target 1774-01-25 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, asthma monograph (2026); Global Strategy for Asthma Management and Prevention, 2025 update (2025); 2020 Focused Updates to the Asthma Management Guidelines: A Report from the National Asthma Education and Prevention Program Coordinating Committee Expert Panel Working Group (2020); Motion Sickness (CDC Yellow Book: Health Information for International Travel) (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit before she sails. Asthma review on her return in the autumn, sooner by message if needed. Next scheduled visit 1773-04-27: periodic preventive visit before a long sea journey. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
